{
  "summary": "Replaces CWallet's mapMasterKeys map and ID counter with a single optional CMasterKey. Key unlocking, passphrase changes, and database write routines now operate directly on this single key rather than looping over a map. Wallets with more than one master encryption key record now fail to load as corrupt.",
  "problem": "The wallet has historically stored master encryption keys in a map to support multiple passphrases, but that feature was never implemented. Existing wallet code inconsistently assumed a single key while still maintaining loops and ID tracking across unlock and passphrase-change routines.",
  "evidence": [
    "PR description notes mapMasterKeys was added in #352 (2011) without clear rationale and 'current code enforces in a few places (inconsistently) that there is only one encryption key'",
    "PR description argues 'no one should have a wallet that has more than one encryption key' and that loading multiple keys should be treated as database corruption"
  ],
